{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"A change of control of a US ETF’s principal adviser generally constitutes an **“assignment”** under the Investment Company Act of 1940. The existing advisory contract must provide for **automatic termination upon assignment**; it does not simply transfer to the new owner.\n\nAbsent applicable exemptive relief, a new principal advisory contract requires:\n\n- **Board approval**, including approval by a majority of independent directors/trustees under Section 15(c).\n- **ETF shareholder approval** by the Act’s defined “majority of outstanding voting securities”—not merely a simple majority of votes cast.\n\nThese requirements arise under Sections 2(a)(4), 15(a), and 15(c).\n\n**Worth knowing:** Rule 15a-4 can permit an interim advisory contract for up to 150 days without prior shareholder approval, subject to specific conditions; it is not a permanent substitute for approval."}}
